        <div class="bg-amber-50/30 dark:bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700 rounded-xl p-6 sm:p-10 font-serif leading-relaxed text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200 text-sm sm:text-base space-y-8 shadow-inner">

          <section class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold font-sans text-navy-900 dark:text-gold-400 border-b border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 pb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="bg-navy-900 text-white text-xs px-2 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">I</span>
              EXECUTIVE SUMMARY & STATUTORY BASIS
            </h3>
            <p>
              <strong>1.</strong> This application respectfully requests the issuance of a Temporary Resident Permit (TRP) pursuant to Subsection 24(1) of the <em>Immigration and Refugee Protection Act</em> (IRPA). The Applicant, <strong>Justin Louis Hardy FASSIO</strong>, is a citizen of the United States of America and the equal joint-guardian of a six-year-old Canadian Citizen child, <strong>Ava FASSIO</strong>, who resides in Kelowna, British Columbia.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>2.</strong> The issuance of a TRP is justified by compelling humanitarian factors, specifically the <strong>Best Interests of the Child (BIOC)</strong> as articulated by the Supreme Court of Canada in <em>Kanthasamy v. Canada (Citizenship and Immigration)</em>, 2015 SCC 61, weighed against a technical non-compliance finding recorded under IRPA ss. 41(a) and 20(1)(a) at the Osoyoos Port of Entry on June 3, 2026.
            </p>
            <div class="p-4 bg-navy-50 dark:bg-navy-950/60 border-l-4 border-navy-700 dark:border-gold-500 rounded text-xs font-sans space-y-2">
              <span class="font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-gold-300 uppercase">Statutory Balancing Framework (IRPA s. 24(1)):</span>
              <p class="text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
                An officer evaluating a TRP request must balance the <strong>compelling need</strong> of the applicant to enter or remain in Canada against any <strong>risk</strong> to Canadian society or program integrity (CBSA Operational Manual ENF 23). The Applicant presents zero criminality, an unbroken history of voluntary compliance, and substantial financial self-sufficiency.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold font-sans text-navy-900 dark:text-gold-400 border-b border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 pb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="bg-navy-900 text-white text-xs px-2 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">II</span>
              THE LEGISLATIVE VOID & THE CUSTODY PARADOX
            </h3>
            <p>
              <strong>3.</strong> <u>The Relocation Prohibition:</u> Under a binding Order of the Supreme Court of British Columbia (Kelowna Registry, File No. 139323, Term 16), Ava FASSIO is legally prohibited from being relocated outside the province of British Columbia. The Applicant and the child's mother share 50/50 joint physical custody and guardianship.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>4.</strong> <u>The Legislative Void:</u> Canadian immigration legislation contains no dedicated temporary or permanent visa stream for foreign national parents of minor Canadian citizen children who are bound by Canadian family court mandates.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>5.</strong> <u>The Custody Paradox:</u> Refusing entry to the father creates a structural paradox. The Supreme Court of British Columbia mandates that the child remain in Kelowna with equal access to both parents, while the immigration authority excludes the father from the country. This paradox directly violates BIOC principles by stripping a 6-year-old Canadian child of her court-ordered equal parent.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold font-sans text-navy-900 dark:text-gold-400 border-b border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 pb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="bg-navy-900 text-white text-xs px-2 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">III</span>
              REBUTTAL OF CBSA GCMS FINDINGS (FILE P-2026-30692)
            </h3>

            <div class="space-y-4 font-sans text-xs sm:text-sm">
              <div class="p-3 border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 rounded bg-white dark:bg-slate-800">
                <span class="font-bold text-navy-800 dark:text-gold-300">1. Uncodified Legal Threshold:</span>
                <p class="mt-1 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
                  The inspecting officer at Osoyoos POE explicitly stated in GCMS notes that an applicant <em>"must spend the majority of his time outside of Canada."</em> This requirement appears nowhere in the IRPA or IRPR. Applying an uncodified, informal "rule of thumb" constitutes an error of law and a fettering of discretion (<em>Stemijon Investments Ltd. v. Canada</em>, 2011 FCA 299).
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="p-3 border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 rounded bg-white dark:bg-slate-800">
                <span class="font-bold text-navy-800 dark:text-gold-300">2. Statutory Dual Intent (IRPA s. 22(2)):</span>
                <p class="mt-1 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
                  Holding a desire to secure permanent residence in the future does not preclude temporary entry (<em>Jewell v. Canada</em>, 2015 FC 1046; <em>Williams v. Canada</em>, 2020 FC 8). The Applicant's prior 2021 Express Entry profile (E002017531) demonstrates transparent, lawful aspirations, not non-compliant intent.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="p-3 border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 rounded bg-white dark:bg-slate-800">
                <span class="font-bold text-navy-800 dark:text-gold-300">3. Procedural Defect in s. 41(a) Charge:</span>
                <p class="mt-1 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
                  The primary GCMS charge under IRPA s. 41(a) contains a blank "specifically:" clause. Leaving the core operational field blank undercuts administrative notice and procedural fairness under <em>Vavilov</em>.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="p-3 border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 rounded bg-white dark:bg-slate-800">
                <span class="font-bold text-navy-800 dark:text-gold-300">4. Misconstrued U.S. Domicile & Fiduciary Ties:</span>
                <p class="mt-1 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
                  The officer erroneously noted "few U.S. ties" while ignoring the Applicant's active Power of Attorney and primary beneficiary status under a California Revocable Living Trust holding $2.3M in Aptos real estate, alongside VA disability pension ties.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="p-3 border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 rounded bg-white dark:bg-slate-800">
                <span class="font-bold text-navy-800 dark:text-gold-300">5. Unbroken 100% Compliance History:</span>
                <p class="mt-1 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
                  The Applicant departed Canada immediately upon his September 23, 2024 VR extension refusal (due to strike-related passport delays) and voluntarily signed an Allowed to Leave (IMM 1282) on June 3, 2026. He has zero history of overstaying.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold font-sans text-navy-900 dark:text-gold-400 border-b border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 pb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="bg-navy-900 text-white text-xs px-2 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">IV</span>
              CONCLUSION & RELIEF SOUGHT
            </h3>
            <p>
              <strong>6.</strong> The Applicant meets all statutory criteria for a Temporary Resident Permit under IRPA s. 24(1). The compelling need to parent a minor Canadian child under a Supreme Court mandate far outweighs any administrative program-integrity risk.
            </p>
